insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 1, 'Three probe decks on the Designer''s default project, priced at 49.990000, 50.000000 and 50.010000 USD per bbl, are run against the PIA royalty tiers of 0 USD/bbl at 7.5 percent and 50 USD/bbl at 10 percent. What rate does the deck priced exactly at the threshold pay?', '["0.075000, the lower tier, because a threshold is the price at which the next tier BEGINS and a price merely equal to it has not yet passed into the band above.", "The mean of the two tiers, because the engine interpolates the rate over the last cent of approach to a threshold rather than jumping.", "0.100000, the upper tier, on gross revenue of 198.9889 giving a royalty of 19.8989.", "0.075000, because the walk stops at the first tier whose threshold is not yet strictly exceeded and keeps the rate it was holding."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The comparison in the engine is `oilPrice >= tier.threshold`, so the threshold belongs to the tier above it: 49.990000 pays 14.9214 at 0.075000 while 50.000000 pays 19.8989 at 0.100000.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 2, 'Why can a price multiplier sweep of the default project, run at 0.700000, 0.710000, 0.720000 and 0.800000, not settle which tier owns a threshold?', '["A sweep brackets a threshold and never lands on one: 0.710000 puts the price at 49.700000 and 0.720000 puts it at 50.400000, so no swept point is ever the threshold itself.", "The sweep changes gross revenue at the same time as the rate, so the implied rate of royalty over gross revenue is contaminated and cannot be read.", "The sweep reports the royalty only as a lifetime total, so no single year''s rate can be recovered from it.", "The multiplier is applied after the royalty is charged, so the tier a swept point selects belongs to the unswept deck price and not to the price printed."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The implied rate steps from 0.075000 to 0.100000 somewhere between the multipliers 0.710000 and 0.720000, and a bracket names an interval rather than a side.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 3, 'A reader tries to place the default project''s 70 USD per bbl deck exactly on a 50 USD per bbl threshold by choosing the multiplier 50 divided by 70. The sweep prints an applied price of 50.000000 and the implied rate reads 0.075000. What happened?', '["The engine rounds the applied price to six decimals before comparing it with the threshold, and a rounded 50.000000 is compared as a value strictly less than 50.", "The applied price is 49.99999999999998 in binary floating point, strictly below the threshold, and six decimal places of printing hid the shortfall.", "The multiplier is applied to the royalty rate rather than to the price, so the tier was selected from the unmultiplied 70 USD per bbl deck.", "The threshold genuinely belongs to the tier below it, and the three probe decks at 49.990000, 50.000000 and 50.010000 are the exception rather than the rule."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Read a printed price column as a rounding of the price the engine used. The implied rate is the measurement, and 0.075000 says the engine was below 50.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 4, '`price_below_every_threshold` runs the default project at a 0.5 multiplier, putting oil at 35 USD per bbl against tiers of 60 USD/bbl at 12.5 percent and 80 USD/bbl at 15 percent. What royalty rate does the field pay?', '["Zero, because a tier list keyed at 60 and 80 USD per bbl charges nothing until the lower of the two keys is reached by the price.", "The 15 percent rate of the upper tier, because with no threshold reached the walk falls through to the last entry in the list.", "0.125000, but only from the year the deck''s step hold lifts the applied price above the 60 USD/bbl key for the first time.", "0.125000 in every year, which is the first tier''s rate charged as the initialisation before any threshold is reached."]'::jsonb, 3, '`getSlidingScaleRoyalty` initialises to the first tier''s rate before the walk begins, which is why year 1 pays 18.0299 on gross revenue of 144.2389.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 5, 'At a price multiplier of 1.200000 on the default project the PIA royalty charges 32.3089 on gross revenue of 323.0889. How much of that revenue was charged at 7.5 percent?', '["None of it. One tier is selected for the year and its rate is charged on the whole of gross revenue, with nothing left in the lower band.", "The share of revenue attributable to the first 50 USD per bbl of price, with the remainder taken at 10 percent in the manner of an income tax bracket.", "The gas and NGL portion of revenue, which stays in the first tier because only the oil price is allowed to select a tier above it.", "The portion earned before the applied price crossed the threshold, which on a rising deck is the earlier years of the same run."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The implied rate at 1.200000 is 0.100000 on every dollar. A blended answer lands between the two printed royalties and looks reasonable, which is what makes it dangerous.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 6, 'The teaching field ODIDI holds 45 USD per bbl through year 5 and steps to 65 at year 6. Under the PIA royalty its year 5 royalty is 5.7145 on gross revenue of 76.1939 and its year 6 royalty is 9.3986 on 93.9859. What moved?', '["Only the rate, since the oil volume is declining and the deck step merely holds gross revenue flat while the tier above takes over.", "Only the revenue, because a deck that steps inside the life of a field cannot move a tier that was fixed when the regime was loaded.", "Both the revenue and the rate: the deck lifted the price so revenue rose, and the same step carried the price past 50 so the implied rate went from 0.075000 to 0.100000.", "The cost recovery limit released profit oil in that year, and the royalty follows the profit oil upward while the rate itself holds at 0.075000."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Two effects compound in one row. Revenue rises from 76.1939 to 93.9859 and the implied rate rises from 0.075000 to 0.100000, so the royalty rises by more than the price move alone.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 7, '`sliding_royalty_price_deck_crossing` runs a deck of 70 USD per bbl, 75 from year 5 and 80 from year 10 against tiers of 60 USD/bbl at 12.5 percent and 80 USD/bbl at 15 percent. Why does the rate not change in year 5?', '["Because the engine holds the tier chosen in year 1 for the whole of the life once cost recovery has begun against that year''s revenue.", "Because 75 USD per bbl has not reached the 80 USD/bbl threshold, so the deck''s step moves the price without moving the tier.", "Because a rate change is deferred to the year after the deck step, which is why the rate becomes 15 percent in year 10 rather than in year 9.", "Because the deck''s second point is a hold rather than a step, and `getPriceForYear` reads it as a continuation of the 70 USD per bbl point."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rate holds at 12.5 percent until year 9 and becomes 15 percent from year 10. Two schedules run at once, one for price and one for rate, and they change in different years.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 8, 'Under a sliding royalty, what selects the tier for a year?', '["The applied price of the highest-value stream in that year, so a gas-weighted field can be lifted into an upper tier by its Mscf price.", "The boe-weighted average of the oil, gas and NGL prices, converted at 6000 scf per barrel before the thresholds are read.", "Gross revenue for the year, which is why a larger field reaches an upper tier at a price a smaller field would not.", "The applied oil price for that year, and nothing else."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Gas and NGL revenue is charged at whatever rate the oil price chose, and neither the gas price nor the NGL price can move it.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 9, '`price_below_every_threshold` pays 177.3774 million USD of royalty over its life on total revenue of 1419.0195, while contractor net cash flow reaches 80.8085 and NPV at 10 percent is negative 111.7254. What does that show about the instrument?', '["It shows the first tier''s rate is charged only until the project''s cumulative position turns negative, after which the royalty line stops.", "It shows a royalty charged on gross revenue is refunded through the cost recovery pool whenever contractor net cash flow closes below the royalty total.", "It has no relief mechanism: there is no minimum price below which the royalty is waived and no link at all to whether the field is making money.", "It shows the instrument reduces its rate when cost recovery is failing, which is why the rate never rises above the first tier''s 12.5 percent here."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A royalty larger than twice the contractor''s whole lifetime cash flow was collected from a project that destroyed value, and 72.2941 million USD of cost was still unrecovered at the horizon.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 10, 'A sweep prints an applied price column and an implied rate column. Which is the measurement of which side of a threshold the engine was on?', '["The applied price, because the tier is chosen from that price and the rate is only a consequence of the choice already made.", "The implied rate, royalty over gross revenue, because it reports the tier the engine actually selected.", "Neither, since both are derived columns rounded from full precision before printing.", "The applied price, provided it is quoted to six decimals, which is enough places to separate any two thresholds a template uses."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A price printing as 50.000000 can be 49.99999999999998, and the implied rate of 0.075000 is what says so.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 11, 'Across the default project sweep the PIA implied rate is 0.075000 at multipliers 0.500000, 0.600000, 0.700000 and 0.710000, and 0.100000 at 0.720000, 0.800000, 1.000000 and 1.200000. What is that pattern?', '["A step function with one break, flat inside each tier and discontinuous where the applied price crosses 50 USD per bbl.", "A ramp that rises with price throughout, sampled coarsely enough at these eight multipliers that its slope looks flat inside each band.", "A step that repeats at every multiple of the threshold, with a further break at 100 USD per bbl.", "A progressive schedule whose average rate rises with price while the marginal rate holds."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Nothing between two thresholds is interpolated. The rate is 0.100000 at an applied price of 50.400000 and still 0.100000 at 84.000000.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 12, 'The PIA template''s first royalty tier is keyed at 0 USD/bbl. Why does that hide something a reader needs to check?', '["Because a threshold of 0 USD/bbl is skipped by the walk, leaving the 50 USD/bbl tier as the only rate the template can charge.", "Because a zero threshold makes the implied rate undefined in any year the applied oil price is itself below one dollar.", "Because the engine treats a 0 USD/bbl key as an instruction to charge no royalty until the next threshold in the list is reached.", "Because at any positive price the first tier is genuinely reached, so the initialisation to the first tier''s rate never shows itself and the walk looks like an ordinary threshold rule."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On tiers keyed at 60 and 80 USD/bbl the initialisation is visible as 0.125000 charged below every threshold, and that is the entry every tier list has to be checked for.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 13, 'What validation does `getSlidingScaleRoyalty` perform on the tier list it is given?', '["It sorts the list ascending by threshold at load, which is why the walk can be described as keeping the highest threshold reached.", "None. It does not sort the list, does not check that thresholds ascend, and does not warn on duplicates.", "It rejects a list whose rates fall as its thresholds rise.", "It checks the first tier''s threshold is zero or negative, so that some rate is always defined below the list''s lowest key."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The walk keeps the last qualifying tier in list order, which equals the highest threshold reached only while the list is sorted, and every shipped template happens to be sorted.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 14, 'How does the walk in `getSlidingScaleRoyalty` treat a price that has reached two thresholds?', '["It stops at the first qualifying tier and charges that rate, which is why the lowest reached threshold governs the year.", "It sums the rates of every qualifying tier, so a price reaching two tiers pays the total of both rates on gross revenue.", "It keeps the rate of the later qualifying tier in list order, having overwritten the earlier one as it walked past it.", "It compares each qualifying tier against the one it is holding and keeps whichever carries the larger rate."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The walk never compares one qualifying tier against another. What survives is whichever qualifying tier came last in the list.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm01-the-sliding-scale', 15, 'The USA Gulf of Mexico template charges a flat 18.75 percent. What is its implied rate on the default project in year 25?', '["0.187500, the same as in year 1, because a flat royalty carries a single rate and no price can move it.", "Lower than 0.187500, because a flat rate is charged on gross revenue and gross revenue has declined through the life of the field.", "Undefined, since the implied rate can only be computed in a year that carries capex in the same row.", "0.187500 on oil revenue only, with the gas and NGL streams left outside the royalty base."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Year 1 charges 50.9979 million USD on gross revenue of 271.9889, and the ratio reads 0.187500 in year 1 and in year 25 alike.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 1, 'On the Designer''s default project year 1 gross revenue is 271.9889 million USD and royalty is 33.9986. A 100 percent cost recovery limit recovers 237.9903. What is the limit a percent of?', '["Gross revenue, which is why a 100 percent limit is described as recovering the whole of the year''s revenue if the pool is deep enough.", "Revenue after royalty, so a full limit takes everything left once the royalty has been removed.", "The recoverable pool brought forward, so the limit caps the share of the outstanding balance that may be released in any one year.", "Gross revenue less that year''s opex, since opex is recovered first as an operating charge and only capex draws against the stated limit."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The royalty is taken first and never becomes recoverable cost, which is why the year 1 allowance at a full limit is 237.9903 and not 271.9889.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 2, 'The golden note on `capped_5pct_never_recovers` says no payback and IRR 0, while the case records payback in year 3 and an IRR of 54.6792 percent. Why does the project pay back even though its pool never clears?', '["The engine treats the closing pool of 2543.7575 as a receivable and credits it to contractor cash in the year the horizon closes.", "Payback is measured on gross revenue rather than on contractor net cash flow, so a project pays back whether or not any cost is recovered.", "The 5 percent limit loosens automatically once the pool exceeds the capex, releasing the arrears in a single year and producing the year 3 payback.", "Revenue the 5 percent limit will not release as cost oil becomes profit oil instead, and that regime gives the contractor the whole of the profit split, so the capital comes home through the profit share."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Cost recovery is not the only way a contractor is paid back. Total profit oil on that case is 5819.7423 against 3355.3781 on the full limit run, and contractor net cash flow still reaches 1530.0622.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 3, 'On the Suite test project, `capped_5pct_never_recovers` pays total tax of 1745.9227 while `flat_test_project` pays 1006.6134. Why does the tighter cost recovery limit pay more tax?', '["Because cost recovered is not a tax deduction: the base is the contractor''s profit share, and cost the limit refuses to release stays inside the profit oil the split and the tax act on.", "Because an unrecovered pool is added to taxable income as a deemed benefit in the year the horizon closes, which raises the lifetime tax total.", "Because the minimum tax provision binds whenever cost recovery falls short, and the minimum is charged on gross revenue rather than on the profit share.", "Because the tighter limit lengthens the life of the pool, and the tax stack charges its capital uplift on every year a balance is still outstanding."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Recovering 306.3022 instead of 2770.6665 leaves 5819.7423 of profit oil rather than 3355.3781, and the tax follows the profit oil upward.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 4, 'Cost recovered and profit oil are computed from the same base. What does a binding cost recovery limit change?', '["The total available to be shared, since revenue the limit refuses to release as cost oil is held back and paid out in no column of the ledger at all.", "The royalty, because a lower limit leaves a larger residual on which the sliding scale charges its rate a second time.", "Who receives the money and in which year, never how much of it there is, because cost recovered plus profit oil is always revenue after royalty.", "The gross revenue of the year, since the engine reduces the applied price when the pool cannot be drawn down at the stated limit."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Profit oil is revenue after royalty minus cost recovered, floored at zero, so at a 100 percent limit on the default project year 1 profit oil is exactly 0.0000 and cost recovered is 237.9903.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 5, 'The default project is run at cost recovery limits of 50, 80, 90 and 100 percent with nothing else changed. What are the lifetime totals?', '["Each limit recovers a different total, rising steadily from 940.4991 at 50 percent to 941.4436 at 100 percent as the allowance widens.", "The 80, 90 and 100 percent runs are identical, each recovering 941.4436 and closing the pool at 0.0000, while the 50 percent run recovers 940.4991 and leaves 0.9444 unrecovered.", "All four runs recover 941.4436, because the pool always clears inside the 25 year horizon whatever the annual allowance is.", "The 50 and 80 percent runs are identical at 940.4991, and the 90 and 100 percent runs are identical at 941.4436."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Once the pool clears inside the life, the limit that cleared it faster changed when the money arrived and not how much of it arrived. Only the 50 percent run fails to finish.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 6, 'At an 80 percent limit on the default project gross revenue falls from 271.9889 in year 1 to 171.8074 in year 6, while profit oil climbs from 47.5981 to 143.6310. What happened?', '["Production improved in the middle years, which lifts profit oil even as the price deck''s step hold pulls gross revenue down.", "The royalty rate fell as the applied price crossed back below a sliding threshold, leaving more revenue after royalty for the profit oil line.", "The cost recovery limit tightens automatically once the contractor has passed payback, which diverts the allowance into profit oil.", "The pool emptied. While the pool was deep the allowance ate the profit oil, and the moment there was nothing left to recover the profit oil was released."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nothing improved and a balance simply ran out. The check is to read gross revenue beside profit oil: if revenue is falling while profit oil rises, the pool cleared.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 7, 'At an 80 percent limit on the default project, cost recovered runs 190.3922, 171.1239, 153.8100, then 96.6215 in year 4 and 23.6264 in year 5. What is the year 5 figure?', '["That year''s opex and nothing more, because the pool is empty and the only cost left to recover is the cost the year itself incurred.", "The unrecovered balance carried out of year 4, released in full now that the allowance finally exceeds it.", "Eighty percent of that year''s revenue after royalty, which is the whole allowance the limit permits once the early capex has been worked off.", "The straight line capital allowance the engine switches to once the pool has been drawn below the year''s allowance."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Year 4 is the partial draw that empties the pool, and every year after it hands back that year''s opex and passes the rest through as profit oil.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 8, '`capped_5pct_never_recovers` closes with 2543.7575 million USD still in the pool. What happens to it?', '["It is written off against the contractor''s taxable profit share in year 25, which is why that case records a lower tax than the full limit run.", "It is carried to a terminal settlement and added to contractor net cash flow in the final year, which is what makes payback possible in year 3.", "Nothing. Nothing in the engine ever pays it, and the horizon does not extend to let the recovery finish.", "It is converted into a loss carryforward that shelters the profit share in the years that follow, though the engine''s 25 year horizon truncates it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'An unrecovered pool is not a receivable. A reader who adds it back values the case too highly by 2543.7575, and does the same to `capped_40pct` by 399.6419 and to `flat_test_project` by 79.3932.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 9, '`never_recovers_huge_capex` takes the Suite test project to 20000 of capex. Profit oil is 0.0000 in every year and total tax is 0.0000, yet government take is 875.1492. Where did it come from?', '["It is the minimum tax, which is charged on gross revenue whenever the profit share falls to zero and no ordinary tax base exists.", "All of it is royalty, which is charged on gross revenue and has nothing to do with whether any cost was recovered.", "It is the government''s share of the 6126.0445 of cost oil recovered.", "It is a clawback of the earlier years'' cost recovery, triggered when the closing pool of 15724.0151 exceeds the capex actually spent."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Total royalty on that case is 875.1492 and total tax is 0.0000, so the whole of government take is the royalty. That is a royalty seen from the cost recovery side.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 10, 'In year 5 the default project incurs 23.6264 of opex and no capex at all, yet at a 50 percent limit it recovers 83.6287. In year 1 it spends 500.0000 of capex and recovers 118.9951. What is the cost recovered column?', '["The year''s own spend, capped at the allowance, which is why the year 1 figure is so much smaller than the capex that produced it.", "The depreciation charge for the year, computed from the capex over a life the cost recovery limit implies.", "The share of the year''s spend the government has agreed to fund, with the balance left for the contractor to carry at its own risk.", "A draw on a historic balance, so the year that recovers most may be a year that spent almost nothing."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A reader who treats cost recovered as a cost column will double count the whole of the year 1 capex of 500.0000 and then wonder why the totals refuse to close.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 11, 'At a 50 percent cost recovery limit on the default project, cost recovered and profit oil are equal in every early row, 118.9951 and 118.9951 in year 1 and 106.9525 and 106.9525 in year 2. Why?', '["Because the engine splits revenue after royalty evenly between cost oil and profit oil in any year an unrecovered balance is still outstanding against the pool.", "Because profit oil is defined as the mirror of cost recovered, which is an identity of cost recovery and holds at every limit.", "Because the allowance is exactly half of revenue after royalty and the pool is deep enough to fill it, so the other half is what remains as profit oil.", "Because the 50 percent limit is applied twice, once to the pool draw and once again to the profit oil that the draw leaves behind."]'::jsonb, 2, 'It is an arithmetic coincidence of a 50 percent limit and a deep pool, and any other limit breaks it: at 80 percent year 1 recovers 190.3922 and leaves 47.5981.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 12, 'On the default project at limits of 80, 90 and 100 percent the total cost recovered over the life is 941.4436 million USD. What is that number made of?', '["The life''s capex of 500.0000 and its opex of 441.4436, taken together, because every dollar comes back and the limit only decides when.", "The capex of 500.0000 plus the share of opex the allowance was able to release before the pool cleared in year 3 or year 4.", "The undepreciated balance of the capex, since opex is expensed against profit oil rather than entering the recoverable pool at all.", "The lifetime allowance, which is the stated percent of the whole life''s revenue after royalty and is therefore a different total at each of the four limits."]'::jsonb, 0, 'There is no capital allowance, no life, no method and no schedule. Capex enters the pool whole in the year it is spent and leaves when revenue after royalty makes room.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 13, 'What does the cost recovery limit cap?', '["The pool, so a balance can never grow beyond the stated percent of the life''s revenue after royalty.", "The annual draw, and nothing else.", "The total ever recovered, which is why a tight limit leaves a permanent shortfall against the capex spent.", "The share of the year''s revenue after royalty available to the contractor once its profit split has also been applied."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It does not vary with the R factor, the price or the year, and a regime that recovers capex and opex at different limits cannot be expressed at all.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 14, 'Undiscounted contractor net cash flow on the default project is 986.7327 at a 100 percent limit and 986.4494 at a 50 percent limit. What should a reader conclude about a tighter limit?', '["That it makes no difference at all, since an undiscounted total is the only honest measure of what a regime is worth to a contractor.", "That it costs the contractor money in proportion to the tightening, which the undiscounted totals understate because they are nominal.", "That it costs the contractor time rather than money, right up to the point where the pool fails to clear inside the horizon.", "That the two figures differ only by the engine''s own arithmetic noise."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The shape of the early years changed and a discounted measure is where that shows. The only lasting difference here is the 0.9444 the 50 percent run leaves unrecovered.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm02-cost-recovery', 15, 'At the close of year 3 on the default project the pool stands at 264.6871 at a 50 percent limit, 71.4398 at 80 percent, 7.0241 at 90 percent and 0.0000 at 100 percent. Which limits have cleared by the end of year 4?', '["Only the 100 percent run, because a limit below a full one leaves a residue that is worked off across the remaining years of the life.", "The 90 and 100 percent runs, since the 80 percent balance of 71.4398 is too large for a single year''s allowance to absorb.", "All four, because the 50 percent run''s remaining 203.4615 is released once the year 4 allowance is applied to revenue after royalty.", "The 80, 90 and 100 percent runs, since the 90 and 80 percent balances of 7.0241 and 71.4398 are both drawn down inside year 4."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The 50 percent run is still carrying 43.9427 at the close of year 7 and finishes the 25 years with 0.9444 unrecovered.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 1, 'How does the engine compute the R factor for a year?', '["Cumulative gross revenue divided by cumulative capex plus opex, both running totals taken through and including that year, and the division is done before the split for the year is chosen.", "That year''s gross revenue divided by that year''s capex plus opex, which is why a year with no capex at all can carry a very large ratio.", "Cumulative gross revenue after royalty divided by cumulative cost, so the royalty instrument moves the ratio and the tranche it selects.", "Cumulative revenue through the previous year divided by cumulative cost through the previous year, so the split is set before the year is priced."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is a ratio of cumulatives that already contains the year being priced. On the default project year 3 the ratio is 1.254640.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 2, 'On the default project year 2 sells 244.4628 million USD of gross revenue against opex of 28.8480 and no capex at all, yet the R factor reads 0.922481. Why is it below 1?', '["Because the R factor is computed from revenue after royalty, and the royalty charged in years 1 and 2 has pushed the numerator below the cost total.", "Because a year carrying no capex is scored on its opex alone, and the ratio is floored at last year''s value.", "Because the 500.0000 of year 1 capex is permanently in the denominator, and cumulative revenue has not yet overtaken cumulative cost.", "Because the ratio is discounted before it is compared with a threshold, and two years of discounting at 10 percent hold the year 2 value below 1."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A year ratio would be enormous here. The R factor crosses 1 when cumulative revenue overtakes cumulative cost, which on this project is year 3.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 3, 'The golden note on `rfactor_tranche_crossing` says the R factor walks through 1.0 in year 3 and the contractor split steps there. The table reads 0.781480 in year 1, 1.383252 in year 2 and 1.846809 in year 3, with splits of 0.600000, 0.600000 and 0.400000. What is wrong with the note?', '["Nothing is wrong. The split does step in year 3 and 1.0 is a real threshold in the tier list, so the note describes the row correctly.", "The 1.0 crossing happens in year 2 and changes nothing, because 60 percent is already the first tier''s split. The year 3 step to 40 percent is the 1.6 threshold.", "The crossing is in year 2 and the step in year 3, so the note is right about the step but has simply dated the crossing one year late.", "The 1.0 crossing does happen in year 3 exactly as the note says, and the error is that the split it triggers there is 30 percent rather than the 40 percent recorded."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The note dates one event wrongly and credits it with a step another threshold caused. Every number in it appears somewhere in the case, which is why no mechanical sweep catches it.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 4, 'The golden note on `rfactor_falls_back` says the R factor peaks just above 2.5. What do its own numbers say?', '["It peaks at 2.501684 in year 5, and 2.972625 is the peak of the crossing case rather than of this one.", "It peaks at 2.545295 in year 22, the last year before the split steps back up from 30 to 40 percent.", "It peaks at 2.494796 in year 23, which is the value that fails the 2.5 threshold and returns the contractor to the 1.6 tranche.", "It peaks at 2.972625 in year 11. The value 2.501684 is where it crosses 2.5 going up, in year 5."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The peak is nearly half a unit above what the note claims. Key on the table and never on a golden''s prose.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 5, 'On `rfactor_falls_back` the R factor is 2.545295 in year 22 with a split of 0.300000 and 2.494796 in year 23 with a split of 0.400000. What is happening?', '["The engine has detected that the field is approaching its economic limit and applies the relief tranche reserved for the tail of the life.", "The tranche list has been re-entered from the top after the peak, so the walk restarts at 60 percent.", "The ratio has fallen back below the 2.5 threshold, so the walk stops at the 1.6 tier and the contractor''s share steps back UP.", "Profit oil has fallen below the level at which a tranche can be applied, so the engine reverts to the split it used before the crossing."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The R factor is a ratio of cumulatives and it is not monotone. A share given up in year 5 is handed back in year 23, and nothing in the engine says a real contract would usually ratchet.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 6, 'Why does the R factor turn down late in the life of a field?', '["Cumulative revenue is reduced by the royalty and the tax as they accrue, and both keep accruing after the production peak has passed.", "Cumulative revenue grows by a smaller amount every year as production declines, while cumulative cost keeps growing on opex that never stops.", "The denominator is reset at the point the recoverable pool clears, and every year after that adds cost to a base that no longer carries the capex.", "The engine applies its year end discounting to the numerator only, so the ratio decays at the discount rate once revenue stops growing."]'::jsonb, 1, 'On `rfactor_falls_back` the ratio peaks at 2.972625 in year 11 and falls in every year afterwards, reaching 2.395606 by year 25.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 7, '`rfactor_tranche_crossing` and `rfactor_falls_back` run the same tranches of 60 percent at R 1, 40 percent at R 1.6 and 30 percent at R 2.5 on the same project. Why does only one of them fall back?', '["The crossing case ratchets its tranche once reached, and the falling back case has that behaviour switched off by its higher opex figure.", "The crossing case reaches the third tranche a year later, and a tranche entered later in the life has fewer years left in which to be lost again.", "The crossing case has no cost recovery limit binding on it, so its cumulative cost stops growing once the pool clears and the ratio cannot fall.", "The crossing case peaks at 3.109087 in year 12 and has only declined to 2.654466 by year 25, which is still above 2.5, so its split never leaves the 30 percent tranche."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The only differences are capex 350 against 320 and opex 20 against 25. A reversal is an accident of where the ratio settles, not a property one case has and the other lacks.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 8, 'On the default project year 1 the R factor is 0.512217, below every threshold in the PIA tranche list. What contractor split is applied?', '["0.600000, the first tier''s split, used as the initialisation before any threshold has been reached.", "No split at all, since a tranche list cannot select a rate until its lowest threshold has actually been reached by the ratio.", "0.300000, because the walk falls through a list none of whose thresholds are met and keeps the split carried by the last entry.", "A split interpolated between zero and the first tier''s 60 percent in proportion to how far the ratio has climbed toward 1."]'::jsonb, 0, '`getTieredSplit` sets the split to the first tier''s split before the walk begins, exactly as the sliding royalty initialises to its first tier''s rate.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 9, 'In year 5 on the default project the PIA tranches give the contractor 59.3639 out of profit oil of 148.4098, at an R factor of 1.769792. How was that computed?', '["The first 1.6 of the ratio was priced at the 60 percent rate and the balance of it at 40 percent, in the manner of a marginal income tax band.", "The profit oil was apportioned between the tranches in proportion to the years spent in each, then split at the two rates.", "The whole of the 148.4098 was split at 0.400000, the rate the 1.6 tranche carries, with no part of it left at the rate below.", "The 40 percent rate was applied to profit oil after the year''s cost recovery draw had been taken out of it a second time."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The tranches are not marginal bands. A blended answer lands above 59.3639 and below what a 60 percent rate would give, which is the most plausible-looking wrong answer available.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 10, 'On `rfactor_tranche_crossing` the contractor''s split falls from 0.600000 in year 2 to 0.400000 in year 3, and profit oil in those years is 46.2528 and 179.0750. What does year 3 show?', '["That the engine compensates a tranche crossing by releasing profit oil in the same year, so the contractor is held whole across the step.", "A split rate and a share of money are different quantities, and the contractor is better off on a lower rate because the pool cleared and released profit oil.", "That the split is applied to cumulative profit oil rather than to the year''s, which is why a lower rate can return a larger amount.", "That the crossing was mis-timed by one year, since a genuine step down would have reduced the contractor''s share of the ledger."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The cost recovery pool cleared and the tranche crossing landed in the same year. Two things moved in opposite directions in one row.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 11, 'In year 11 on the default project the R factor is 2.581420, which has reached all three PIA thresholds, and the implied split reads 0.300000 on profit oil of 80.0217. What would happen if the 2.5 tranche were listed first?', '["Nothing, because the engine sorts the tier list ascending by threshold when the regime is loaded, so the stated order of the tranches is only presentational.", "The engine would refuse the regime, since a tier list whose thresholds do not ascend fails validation before any year is computed.", "The walk would keep the highest threshold reached, which is 2.5 whatever the order, so the split would still read 0.300000 in that row.", "The walk would keep whichever qualifying tier came last in the reordered list, so 0.600000 or 0.400000 would be charged, and no error would be raised."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The walk keeps the LAST qualifying tier in list order, which equals the highest threshold reached only while the list is sorted. No gate in the app checks the order.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 12, 'On `harsh_split_40_royalty_20` the year 1 profit oil is 0.0000 and the implied split is null. What does that mean?', '["The engine chose a split for that year as it does for every year. It simply had nothing to apply it to, and no ratio of a share to zero can recover which one it chose.", "The engine declined to select a tranche because the R factor of 0.793622 had not reached the first threshold in the list.", "The split was recorded as null because a flat 40 percent regime carries no tranche list for the walk to select from at all.", "The year was excluded from the split calculation entirely, which is why the R factor for that year is also not reported."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The implied split is read back out of the ledger as a ratio, and years 20 to 25 of that case are null for the same reason: profit oil is 0.0000.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 13, 'Someone reads the `rfactor_tranche_crossing` table looking for the row where the R factor equals 1.6. What will they find?', '["The year 3 row, since the engine snaps the R factor to the threshold value in the year a crossing is recognised, which is how the step is dated.", "The year 2 row at 1.383252, which the engine rounds up to the threshold for the purpose of choosing the tranche.", "No such row. The ratio moves in jumps the size of the year''s revenue and cost, and 1.6 sits in the gap between 1.383252 and 1.846809.", "Two rows, one where the ratio crosses each threshold going up and one where it crosses that same threshold coming back down later in the life."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A crossing is identified by the pair of rows that straddle it, never by a row that lands on it, and the ledger prints no marker on a crossing year.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 14, 'The R factor column on the default project is identical under the USA Gulf of Mexico template and under Nigeria PIA. What does that say about it?', '["It is cached from the first template loaded in a comparison run, which is why the six templates report the same column.", "It responds only to production, price and cost, so no royalty, cost recovery limit, split or tax setting can move it.", "It is computed from the contractor''s own position, and the two templates happen to leave the contractor in the same place on this project.", "It is fixed by the Designer''s default project rather than by the regime, and a different project would make the two columns diverge."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Neither cumulative depends on the regime. Royalty is not subtracted from the revenue side and tax is not added to the cost side.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm03-the-r-factor', 15, 'What can the R factor instrument not be told to do?', '["Select a tranche from a threshold expressed as a ratio rather than as an oil price, which is what a sliding royalty does instead.", "Choose a split from the year''s own ratio, since the engine reads the previous year''s ratio and applies it with a one year lag.", "Change the split it applies partway through a life, which is why a tranche once selected governs every year that follows it.", "See time value, ignore late revenue, or exclude any cost from either running total."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Revenue in year 1 and revenue in year 25 enter the numerator at face value, so a slow project and a fast one with the same totals share an R factor.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 1, 'Corporate income tax and the resource rent tax in this model are charged on which quantity?', '["Gross revenue, taken before any cost at all.", "Revenue after royalty less cost recovered, which is the whole of profit oil before the split takes the government''s part out of it.", "The contractor''s share of profit oil, and nothing else.", "The contractor''s profit share less half of that year''s opex, the deduction the engine''s own header describes."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Cost is compensated through cost recovery and is never deducted again at the tax line, and the header records that the halving of opex was invented and has been removed. In year 3 of the Brazil instruments the base is 75.7962 million USD and the charge is 25.7707.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 2, 'The Brazil instruments on the Designer''s default project sell 271.9889 million USD of gross revenue in year 1 and charge 0.0000 of tax. What produced the zero?', '["A 100 percent recovery limit took the whole of that year''s revenue after royalty back against the 500.0000 million USD of capex, so profit oil and therefore the base were 0.0000.", "The capex of 500.0000 million USD was written off against income in the year it was spent, which is the capital allowance the model grants in the first year only.", "A loss in year 1 was carried forward, so nothing was charged until the carried balance had been worked off in year 3.", "The stack holds a two year tax holiday before its first charge, which is why the first tax in the column arrives in year 3."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Profit oil reads 0.0000 in years 1 and 2 and the base is the profit share, so the first charge is the 25.7707 million USD of year 3.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 3, 'Running the Brazil instruments with two of the three tax instruments switched off gives 502.1091 million USD for a corporate income tax of 34 percent and 62.5794 for a resource rent tax of 40 percent, on the same base. Why does the higher rate collect the smaller amount?', '["The resource rent tax is charged on what survives the corporate income tax, so it works on a base that the 34 percent charge has already taken a third out of, which is how a second profits tax is usually stacked on a first.", "The resource rent tax falls on the government''s share of profit oil rather than the contractor''s, and on this regime that share is much the smaller of the two.", "The resource rent tax begins only once the cost recovery pool has cleared, so it reaches fewer of the 25 years than the corporate income tax does.", "The resource rent tax base is the profit share less 20 percent of the whole 500.0000 million USD of capex, subtracted in every year, so most years have no positive base left."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The uplift removes the same fixed amount every year, so the resource rent tax alone is back to 0.0000 by year 9 while profit oil is still 93.4441 million USD.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 4, 'The tax charged in a year is the larger of the corporate income tax plus the resource rent tax, and the minimum tax. In year 1 of the Brazil decomposition a 5 percent minimum reads 13.5994 million USD and the published stack reads 0.0000. What would switching the minimum on charge in that year?', '["Nothing, because a minimum tax is waived in any year whose profit share is 0.0000 and the base test governs all three instruments alike.", "13.5994 million USD, because the minimum replaces the profits taxes rather than adding to them.", "13.5994 million USD on top of the published stack, since a floor is an additional instrument and the three charges are summed before the larger is taken.", "A share of 13.5994 million USD set by how far the floor stands above the profits taxes."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rule is a maximum and not a sum. From year 3 the stack reads 25.7707 against a minimum of 10.9864 and the floor never binds again.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 5, 'The decomposition prints 502.1091 million USD for the corporate income tax alone and 62.5794 for the resource rent tax alone, against a published stack of 564.6884. What should a reader conclude from adding the first two figures?', '["That the printed columns disagree with the stack, which is evidence that the maximum rule drops part of one instrument somewhere in the life of the field.", "That a third instrument is quietly present in the published run, since a stack built from two exact components would reproduce its own total at the printed precision.", "Nothing at all, because each column total is rounded on its own; unrounded, the two agree with 564.6884366927 to within 1.1369e-13.", "That the decomposition is only approximate inside the engine."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The decomposition is exact in the engine and only approximate at the precision printed, so quote the columns and never assert that the printed figures add.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 6, 'The uplift sweep on the Brazil instruments reports a total tax of 590.7165 million USD at an uplift of 0 percent. What is that figure?', '["The resource rent tax alone at 40 percent, because the sweep sets the template''s own corporate income tax of 34 percent to zero in every one of its rows.", "The regime''s whole tax bill with the relief withdrawn, which is what the published stack of 564.6884 million USD becomes when the uplift is taken away.", "The corporate income tax and the resource rent tax added together on a base that no longer carries any capital relief at all.", "The total government take at that uplift, of which the tax column is the part collected through the stack rather than through the royalty."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The published Brazil stack on this project is 564.6884 million USD. The sweep''s tax, cash flow, take and NPV columns are an isolation of one instrument and not the regime.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 7, 'What does the uplift sweep report at an uplift of 50 percent?', '["A total tax of 1.0282 million USD, the smallest charge in the sweep, arriving in year 4 as it does at an uplift of 30 percent.", "A total tax of 0.0000 and no year in the whole life carrying a positive charge.", "A negative tax, because the base falls below zero and the engine credits the shortfall back to the contractor as relief in that year.", "A refusal, because an uplift larger than the resource rent tax rate is rejected by the engine as an instrument that cannot be expressed."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Half of the 500.0000 million USD of capex is removed from the base in every year, and contractor net cash flow reaches 1476.7914 million USD.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 8, 'The resource rent tax uplift is a percent of total capex. How often is it subtracted from the base?', '["Once, in the year the capex is spent, which is why no charge can arise in year 1 on a project that spends its capital there.", "In every year until the cumulative relief given has reached the whole of the capex, after which the subtraction stops and the full base is charged.", "In every year the cost recovery pool still carries a balance, since the uplift is the tax side of the same unrecovered capital.", "In every one of the 25 years."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At the default 20 percent on 500.0000 million USD of capex the relief given over the life is five times the whole capital cost, which the engine''s own comment calls a screening approximation.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 9, 'Two published cases run the same regime on the same project and differ only in the uplift field: one omits it and one sets it to 0. Total tax is 1638.5664 million USD against 2607.5549. What decided the gap?', '["The omitted field took the documented default of 20 percent, so one run carries a relief the other does not.", "The omitted field was read as zero and the explicit zero as missing, so the two case names sit against the wrong totals and the pair should be read the other way round.", "The two runs differ in their corporate income tax as well, because the default uplift is applied to that rate before the resource rent tax is worked out.", "A minimum tax bound in the second run and replaced the profits taxes."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Contractor net cash flow is 1812.4483 against 843.4598 million USD. The golden''s note on the second case spells the parameter rrtUpliptPct while the engine reads rrtUpliftPct, which is why the numbers and not the prose are the record.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 10, 'In the published case where the minimum tax binds, year 1 has gross revenue of 876.0000 million USD, profit oil of 0.0000 and a tax of 87.6000. What does that row establish?', '["The minimum tax is charged on the profit share like the other two, and the 0.0000 in that row is the printing of a very small positive base.", "The minimum tax is charged on revenue after royalty and not on gross revenue.", "The minimum tax is a percent of gross revenue and takes no notice of the profit share, the recovery limit or the unrecovered pool.", "The minimum tax is charged only in years where the profits taxes come to nothing, which is what makes it a floor rather than a tax of its own."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Over the life the instrument collects 700.1194 million USD and leaves contractor net cash flow at 2575.8655.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 11, 'A year in which the minimum tax exceeds the profits taxes pays more than those taxes would have charged. What becomes of the difference?', '["It is carried forward and set against the corporate income tax of the first later year in which the profits taxes are the larger of the two figures.", "Nothing. There is no minimum tax credit, and the excess is not banked against any later year.", "It is added to the unrecovered cost pool, which is where the model parks any amount it cannot settle inside the year that produced it.", "It is settled at the horizon, because the stack is reconciled on lifetime totals such as the 700.1194 million USD that case collects."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The instrument has no credit, no exemption for a loss making year and no sight of the royalty already taken off the same gross revenue.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 12, 'Ghana - Deepwater charges a corporate income tax of 35 percent on the default project and collects 230.9340 million USD, while Generic Royalty/Tax charges 30 percent and collects 422.8854. What explains that order?', '["Ghana''s royalty of 134.3464 million USD is deducted from the base before the rate is applied to it, and Generic''s larger royalty is not deducted at all.", "Ghana recovers far more of its cost, so much less of the revenue after royalty survives as profit oil for the higher rate to reach.", "Ghana''s rate is charged on the contractor''s net cash flow rather than on the profit share, and that is a smaller quantity in every year.", "The base. Generic hands the contractor the whole of profit oil, while Ghana splits it on the R factor and the government''s own share never passes through the tax line."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Government take runs the other way for exactly that reason, 1316.6067 million USD for Ghana against 758.7514 for Generic.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 13, 'Nigeria - PIA (2021) collects the smallest total tax of the four templates carrying a corporate income tax alone, 174.0882 million USD, inside the largest government take of the four, 1339.2784. What does that pair say?', '["The template''s royalty must be the largest of the four, because a small tax inside a large take can only be made up by a charge on gross revenue.", "The tax line measures one instrument and the take measures the regime, and on one ledger the two can point in opposite directions.", "The take is the wrong column to read, since it includes the contractor''s own profit share.", "The tax total is understated, because the government''s share of profit oil is a tax collected in kind and belongs in that column."]'::jsonb, 1, 'USA - Gulf of Mexico collects 260.7539 million USD of tax inside a take of 764.5528, so ranking these regimes by the tax column reverses the ranking by take.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 14, 'By what route does capex reach the tax computation?', '["Through cost recovery, since the 941.4436 million USD recovered on the default project is subtracted from the profit share before any rate is applied.", "Through a capital allowance written down across the 25 year horizon, which is the depreciation the four tax fields between them express.", "Through the resource rent tax uplift only, and four of the six templates set that rate to 0, so for those four capex reaches the tax line by no route at all.", "Through the cost recovery limit, which fixes how much of the capital has been returned and therefore how much of it is still deductible at the tax line."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The base is the profit share and recovery has already been settled in cash, so no part of the 941.4436 million USD appears in the tax computation.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm04-the-tax-stack', 15, 'Years 1 and 2 of the Brazil instruments have profit oil of 0.0000 and pay 0.0000 of tax, and year 3 is then charged 25.7707 million USD on a base of 75.7962. What did those two years buy?', '["Nothing. There is no loss carryforward, so a year whose base is not positive pays nothing and passes nothing to the year after it.", "Relief in year 3, which is why that year''s charge is smaller than the rate of 34 percent applied to a base of 75.7962 would otherwise have been.", "A balance in the unrecovered cost pool, which is the model''s route for a base that cannot be used in the year that produced it.", "A deferral, since the stack is settled again on lifetime totals when the 25 year horizon closes on the ledger."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The year 3 charge is the full rate on 75.7962, and the corporate income tax alone over the life comes to 502.1091 million USD.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 1, 'In this engine each year''s contractor net cash flow is divided by one plus the rate raised to which power?', '["The year number less one, so the first row enters at face value the way an initial investment does in a spreadsheet laid out from column zero.", "The year''s own number, so year 1 is already discounted once and there is no time zero row anywhere in the ledger.", "The year number less one half, which is the mid period convention the screening engine in the same package applies to its own rows.", "The year number, but only from the first year with a positive cash flow."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A row is a year and the year is the power. On USA - Gulf of Mexico over the default project the year 1 flow of negative 310.0117 million USD enters the sum after one division.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 2, 'The swept present value of USA - Gulf of Mexico on the default project reads 980.9313 million USD at a rate of 0 percent. What check does that figure provide?', '["It is contractor net cash flow and government take added together, since at a rate of zero the two are no longer separated by any timing.", "It shows a rate of zero is handled as a special case in the code, which is why the sweep can begin below the project''s own rate at all.", "It is the mid year figure at 10 percent, because the parity factor of 1.048809 leaves the two conventions agreeing at the foot of the sweep.", "It has to equal the undiscounted total contractor net cash flow of that ledger, and it does."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A discount rate of zero is not a special case; it simply makes every divisor one. A top row that does not match the ledger total is reading different flows, because at a rate of zero every discounting convention returns the same sum.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 3, 'A reader lays the ledger out with the year 1 outflow at time zero and leaves it undiscounted. What does that do to a swept table of present values?', '["Every value comes out too high, and too high by a factor that changes with the rate, so no single correction repairs the whole table.", "Only the value at a rate of 0 percent moves, since every other row divides the same 310.0117 million USD by a number larger than one.", "Every value comes out too low, because a cost at face value is larger than one divided by one plus the rate.", "Nothing moves at all, because the convention governs receipts and the year 1 flow is an outlay."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Laying the ledger out a year early multiplies every discounted value by one plus the rate, so the overstatement grows with the rate and no single correction repairs a swept table. The row at 0 percent is the one exception, because the factor there is one and the value stays at the ledger''s own total of 980.9313 million USD.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 4, 'This engine discounts year end and the screening engine in the same package discounts mid year. On identical cash flows, how do the two present values stand to each other?', '["The mid year figure is larger by half a year of discounting on the first row alone, since that is the only row whose placing the convention moves.", "The mid year figure is larger by an amount set by the shape of the cash flows.", "The mid year figure is larger by exactly the square root of one plus the rate, which is the factor 1.048809 at 10 percent.", "The mid year figure is the smaller of the two, because placing a flow in the middle of a year adds the half year of discounting that a year end convention leaves out."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The relation is pinned by a parity gate in the engines test file. It holds only on identical cash flows, so it checks the discounting and never the fiscal terms: the flat regime''s 1262.3470 million USD at 10 percent times that factor reproduces the mid year figure.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 5, 'The ratio of the mid year present value to the year end present value reads 1.048809 at 10 percent and 1.140175 at 30 percent. What would that column read on a field producing twice as much oil?', '["Twice the values at every rate, since both present values double and the ratio between them doubles with them.", "The same values, because the ratio depends on the rate alone and on nothing in the cash flows.", "Values nearer 1.000000, because a larger ledger is dominated by its later years, where the two conventions differ least.", "Values that cannot be known without rerunning both engines, since the ratio is a property of the one ledger the parity gate was written against."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At 0 percent the two conventions agree exactly at 980.9313 million USD and the ratio is 1.000000. The column grows with the rate, from 1.024695 at 5 percent to 1.118034 at 25.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 6, 'What does the discounting in this engine decline to give a reader?', '["A figure at a rate of 0 percent, because the code guards against a divisor of one and hands back the undiscounted total in its place.", "A figure above 30 percent, the top of the swept range and the highest rate the function accepts.", "A figure for a regime whose net cash flow is negative in its early years, which it reports as 0.0000 rather than as a present value.", "A discounted government take or a discounted tax, since it discounts contractor net cash flow and nothing else."]'::jsonb, 3, 'There is no valuation year, no partial period and no inflation term, and the horizon is 25 rows for every regime and every project.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 7, 'All six templates return a negative present value on the teaching field at 12 percent, Brazil - Concession the least negative at negative 5.8662 million USD. What does that say about Brazil on that field?', '["The field loses money under that regime, as a negative present value reports.", "Its payback in year 6 falls outside the reach of the discounting, so the years after it contribute nothing to the sum at all.", "Its internal rate of return is 11.4055 percent, which is below 12, and that is the whole of the explanation.", "Its undiscounted contractor net cash flow of 192.9896 million USD never covers the capital, so the project fails to return its outlay."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Brazil returns 192.9896 million USD of undiscounted contractor net cash flow and pays back in year 6. A negative present value at a rate above the project''s own return is not a loss.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 8, 'The solver checks for a sign change, checks the value at a rate of zero, brackets by doubling from 100 percent ten times, then bisects 80 times. Which of those steps produces a reported 102400.0000 percent?', '["The bracketing, which reports its own bound when the present value is still positive at the top of the doubling.", "The bisection, which converges on the largest rate it can represent when the two ends of the bracket never change sign between them.", "The sign change check, which returns the top of the search rather than 0.", "The check at a rate of zero, which substitutes the bound whenever the present value there is very large, as it is on the case whose value at 10 percent is 1651.9835."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Eighty halvings is a fine sieve, so the engine and the golden agree to four decimals wherever a root exists. A bound is what arrives when no root was found inside the search.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 9, 'The published case with flows of negative 1 then 2000 reports an internal rate of return of 102400.0000 percent. What is that number?', '["It is the root of those flows to four decimals, which is enormous because a single receipt of 2000 stands against an outlay of 1.", "It is 100 doubled ten times, the top of the search, reported because the present value was still positive there. The true root is 199900.0000 percent.", "It is a cap the engine applies to any rate too large for a screening study, in the manner of the 1000 percent clamp the engine next door uses.", "It is the result of 80 bisections inside a bracket that had already been shown to contain the root."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The golden pins the disagreement rather than hiding it: engine 102400.0000 percent against an oracle root of 199900.0000 percent.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 10, 'What separates a rate that came out of 80 bisections from one that is really the edge of the search?', '["The function returns a status flag beside the rate, and a bound carries that flag while a genuine root does not carry it.", "A bound is always reported as 0.0000 percent, which is why a rate of exactly zero is the signal to go back and read the flows by hand.", "A bound is always larger than the largest rate in the discount sweep, so any rate above 30 percent should be treated as one.", "A bisected rate looks like 13.0662 percent, and a bound looks like 102400."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The tell is a suspiciously round number where a rate should be. The capex multiplier of 0.7 case reports 1095.4783 percent and that one is a real root.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 11, 'The solver returns 0.0000 percent for two published cases that could hardly be further apart. Which two?', '["The case whose flows never change sign, and the case whose bracket search climbed to 102400.0000 percent before any root had been found, which is the disagreement the golden pins.", "The case whose flows never change sign, worth 25.6198 million USD at 10 percent, and the case whose only root is negative, at negative 16.5289.", "The case with no capital outlay at all, and the case whose payback year is null because cumulative cash flow never turns positive.", "The case whose present value at a rate of zero is exactly zero, and the case whose present value at 10 percent is exactly zero."]'::jsonb, 1, 'One zero means the project never loses and the other means it never wins, and the function prints the same four characters for both.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 12, 'A reader sees 0.0000 percent in the rate column and reads it as a project that breaks even exactly. What is wrong with that reading?', '["Nothing, provided the present value at a rate of zero is checked as well, because both guards fire on a project sitting at break even.", "Only the precision, since the engine prints four decimals on a value it never obtained by bisection in the first place.", "It is backwards in both directions at once, since one zero is a project that never loses and the other is a project that never wins.", "It confuses the rate with the payout year, which is the first year the R factor rises above 1.0."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The published pair reads 25.6198 million USD at 10 percent against negative 16.5289, and both report 0.0000 percent.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 13, 'What goes wrong when a comparison is sorted or filtered on the internal rate of return column?', '["Every regime reporting 0.0000 percent is left unranked, and a filter set below a threshold hides the hopeless projects and the ones that needed no capital together.", "The sort puts the bound of 102400.0000 percent at the top, which is the only way a bound can be told apart from a root.", "The sort disagrees with a sort on present value, which is the ranking the summary is built on.", "Nothing, so long as the negative rates are read as the losses they stand for before the column is sorted."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The case with capex of 20000 reports 0.0000 percent on a contractor net cash flow of negative 15724.0151 million USD and a present value of negative 15453.8510.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 14, 'The capex multiplier of 0.7 on the flat regime reports an internal rate of return of 1095.4783 percent with a payout year of 1. Is that figure a root?', '["No, it is the doubling bracket reported as a rate, in the same way as the 102400.0000 percent of the published case with flows of negative 1 then 2000.", "Yes. The reduced outlay came back almost immediately, so the rate is real, and it is still not a number anybody can act on.", "No, it is the 1000 percent Newton clamp of the screening engine next door.", "Yes, and it settles the comparison, because a rate that large outranks anything else the summary could put beside it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A large rate is not a good project. The case pays back a reduced outlay in the first year, and the six templates on the default project sit between 28.2129 and 44.6574 percent.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm05-rate-and-return', 15, 'Which statement about what the internal rate of return function hands back is correct?', '["It returns the number of roots it found beside the rate, which is how a reader recognises a curve with more than one of them.", "It returns a negative rate when the only root is negative, which is the case the second guard exists to report faithfully.", "It returns one number and no status, so a guard, a bound and a genuine root arrive at the call site indistinguishable from one another.", "It returns the bracket it searched alongside the rate, so a reported 102400.0000 percent can be read as the bound that it is."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine reports 0.0000 percent rather than a negative rate, so a genuinely loss making project cannot be described by that column at all.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 1, 'A comparison of several regimes on one field is put in front of you. What is written down before any number is read?', '["The headline reading of each regime, which is its present value at the field''s own rate beside its rate of return and its payback year.", "The four instruments of each regime: its royalty, its cost recovery limit, its profit split and its tax stack.", "The field''s own production profile and price deck, which every regime in the comparison shares.", "The order the summary has sorted the regimes into, because that order is the engine''s own verdict on the comparison."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Two lines of instruments predict most of what the ledgers will do. On the teaching field a flat 0 percent royalty, a 50 percent limit and a tiered split already say what kind of ledger Angola - Deepwater PSC will return.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 2, 'Angola - Deepwater PSC carries a flat 0 percent royalty, a 50 percent recovery limit, an R factor split of 70, 50 and 30 percent and a stack of 25 and 50 percent. Which of those leaves 192.1146 million USD in the pool at the close of the teaching field?', '["The flat 0 percent royalty, since a regime that takes nothing off gross revenue has no mechanism for returning capital either.", "The resource rent tax at 50 percent, whose uplift is charged against the pool in every year of the life and holds the balance open.", "The R factor split, which hands the contractor a smaller share of profit oil as the ratio climbs and so slows the return of capital.", "The 50 percent recovery limit."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Angola recovers 618.0991 million USD on that field against 790.8868 for Brazil - Concession, whose limit is 100 percent. The limit decides how fast the pool can drain and nothing else in the list does.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 3, 'Under one template the implied royalty rate on the teaching field reads 0.187500 in year 1 and 0.187500 in year 25. Under another it reads 0.075000 in years 1 to 5 and 0.100000 from year 6. Which reading identifies the instrument?', '["The one that changes inside the life, because a rate that moves mid field is a sliding scale and the year it moves is a price event.", "The one that holds, because only a flat royalty can charge the same rate in year 1 and in year 25 of a declining field.", "Neither, because an implied rate is royalty over gross revenue and that ratio is fixed by the price deck rather than by the tier list.", "The one that changes, because a royalty rate that moves inside a life is following the production decline through the variable opex."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A sliding royalty sitting below every threshold reads as constant as a flat one, so a rate that holds proves nothing. Year 1 pays 26.1856 million USD on 139.6563 and year 25 pays 1.2953 on 6.9081 under the flat 18.75 percent.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 4, 'Ghana - Deepwater recovers 788.5887 million USD on the teaching field and closes with 21.6250 still in the pool. Which of those two figures is read first?', '["The recovery total, since it is the number the ledger reports and the pool is derived from it by subtraction.", "Neither on its own. The two have to be added, because the sum of them is the cost the contractor actually incurred over the life.", "The closing pool, because a recovery total says nothing until you know how much was never paid back.", "The recovery total, because the closing pool is settled against government take at the horizon."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Angola - Deepwater PSC recovers 618.0991 and closes at 192.1146 on the same field. A large closing pool is money the contractor spent that the ledger never paid back.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 5, 'The Professional tier ends on one sentence about instruments. What does it say?', '["An instrument is only as good as the ledger it is read on, so a regime has to be compared on more than one field before it is ranked.", "An instrument''s rate never tells you what it collects. Its base does.", "An instrument''s rate ranks its burden whenever two regimes are run on the same project with the same production and the same price deck.", "An instrument that cannot be expressed in the four fields should be approximated by raising the rate of one that can."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A corporate income tax of 21 percent collected 260.7539 million USD on the default project where one of 35 percent collected 230.9340, because the two rates were charged on different bases.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 6, 'Every one of the six templates returns a negative present value on the teaching field at 12 percent. How is that reported?', '["As one fact, that every rate of return on that field is below 12 percent, rather than as six separate failures.", "As six loss making regimes, since a negative present value on a 25 year ledger is the model''s report of a loss.", "As evidence that the field''s discount rate is set too high, because a rate no regime can beat is not a screening rate.", "As an artefact of year end discounting, which is the more conservative of the two conventions in the package."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Brazil - Concession is the least negative at negative 5.8662 million USD with a rate of return of 11.4055 percent, and it returns 192.9896 undiscounted with payback in year 6.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 7, 'The summary of a comparison is sorted by contractor net present value descending. What can be read off a regime''s position in it?', '["That the last row is the heaviest regime for the contractor, since government take runs in the reverse order of contractor present value.", "That the rows are in order of rate of return as well, because the two measures rank the same cash flows on the same ledger.", "That the rows are in order of payback, since a regime that returns capital faster carries more of its cash in the early discounted years.", "That the first row is the best regime for the contractor, and nothing else."]'::jsonb, 3, 'On the teaching field the sort puts Brazil - Concession first and Angola - Deepwater PSC last, while government take runs the other way at 316.7898 million USD for Angola against 232.9950 for Brazil.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 8, 'A reading is handed over as the figure 192.9896 million USD, and another as negative 5.8662. What is missing from each?', '["The units, since millions of USD and percent cannot sit beside each other.", "The sign convention, because a contractor cash flow and a present value are reported with opposite signs by this engine.", "The regime on the first and the discount rate on the second, and without them neither figure is an answer.", "The year, since both are lifetime totals over a fixed horizon."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both belong to Brazil - Concession on the teaching field, the first as undiscounted contractor net cash flow over the life and the second as its present value at 12 percent.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 9, 'The seven step reading has been worked end to end on a field and every number is labelled. What does the method still refuse to produce?', '["A recommendation. It says what each regime does to this ledger and never which one a government should offer or a contractor should sign.", "A comparison across regimes, since each ledger is worked on its own and the steps do not put two of them side by side.", "A present value, because the method stops at the undiscounted totals and leaves the discounting to a separate reading.", "A reading of the tax stack, since the four fields cannot express what a real fiscal code charges."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The sandbox is not a second fiscal truth. It compares the shape of regimes against each other, and a number lifted out of it and quoted as a liability is worth nothing.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 10, 'The comparison summary carries an effective tax rate, and Brazil - Concession reads 27.5413 percent on the teaching field. What should a reader know about that column before quoting it?', '["It is government take divided by gross revenue, so it is not comparable with a headline tax rate at all.", "A second value for the same quantity, computed on the same cash flows a different way, sits in the same result object and appears on the screen too.", "It is the rate the tax column implies, so it can be checked by dividing total tax by total profit oil on the same ledger.", "It is reported only for regimes whose profit oil is positive in every year, so a comparison of six can carry fewer than six values."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Two effective tax rates live in one result and both are on the screen. Working out which is which is where the next tier starts, and the six on that field run from 27.5413 to 37.4463 percent.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 11, 'The capex sweep on the teaching field runs from a multiplier of 0.8 and stops at 1.4, seven points in all. What does the resilience verdict built on it price?', '["Every overrun the project could suffer, since the sweep is run to the point where the least resilient regime stops paying back.", "The overrun at which the best regime for the contractor changes.", "A cost overrun no larger than the end of that swept range, whatever the axis of the chart appears to promise.", "A cost overrun measured against the closing pool, because an overrun that cannot be recovered is the only kind that reaches the contractor."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Over those points Ghana - Deepwater gives up 150.9021 million USD of contractor present value and Angola - Deepwater PSC 215.1075. Read the axis before the verdict.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 12, 'Across the swept prices on the teaching field the government share climbs 1.9971 points for Ghana - Deepwater and falls 37.6994 for USA - Gulf of Mexico. What does a falling share mean?', '["That the government collects less money as price rises.", "That the regime is charging a sliding royalty in reverse, since only a rate that falls with price can produce a falling share.", "That the sweep has run below the range where the regime''s instruments bind, so the share is falling towards the level it holds at low price.", "That the regime hands the upside to the contractor as price rises."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A share is a ratio and not an amount. The regime whose share climbs is the progressive one, which is why Ghana - Deepwater is named the most responsive to higher prices.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 13, 'The verdict named best for the contractor on the teaching field is Brazil - Concession, whose present value there is negative 5.8662 million USD. What is that verdict saying?', '["That Brazil is the least bad of the six, which is a real answer to a real question and not the question most readers think they asked.", "That Brazil returns a positive present value once the negative sign is read as a convention of the summary column.", "That the verdict is unusable, since a ranking of six negative numbers cannot name a best regime at all.", "That the field is uneconomic under every regime, so the verdict is about the field rather than about Brazil."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every rate of return on that field is below the 12 percent it is discounted at. Being least bad is worth saying out loud, with its label attached.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 14, 'Three habits from the Professional tier carry into a six regime comparison. What are they?', '["Sort on present value, then on rate of return, then on payback, so that a verdict is never made on one column alone.", "Name the base before the rate, read the closing pool before believing a cost recovery total, and write down what a zero means before quoting it.", "Convert every regime to a common effective tax rate, discount at one rate, and report the range rather than a point.", "Check the price deck, the production profile and the cost columns, since a comparison is only as good as the inputs the regimes share."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Each habit answers a way this package has already misled a reader: a 21 percent rate that collected more than a 35 percent one, a recovery total with 2543.7575 million USD left unpaid behind it, and a rate of 0.0000 percent standing for two opposite things.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'module', 'm06-the-professional-reading', 15, 'On the teaching field under the PIA template the pool clears in year 9 and stands at 0.0000 through year 18, then reads 0.5221 million USD in year 19 and 30.5883 at the close of year 25. What reopened it?', '["A second round of capex late in the life, which enters the pool whole in the year it is spent as the first round did.", "The royalty rate stepping up in year 19, which reduced revenue after royalty below the level the limit needs to clear a year''s cost.", "Revenue fell far enough that the allowance no longer covered the year''s own cost, so the shortfall began accruing again.", "The uplift on the resource rent tax, which is charged in every one of the 25 years and is added to the pool in the years the tax is zero."]'::jsonb, 2, 'In year 19 the allowance releases 12.3236 million USD against a cost of 12.8457, and the difference of that year and every year after it is what carries the pool to 30.5883.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 1, 'A tier list keyed at 60 and 80 USD per bbl is run against a deck the price never lifts above 35. What royalty rate is charged?', '["No royalty at all, since the lowest threshold on the list was never reached in any year of the life and a tier nobody reaches charges nothing.", "A rate interpolated between zero and 12.5 percent according to how close the price came to the first threshold, in the way a scale that slides on price is usually taken to work.", "The first tier''s rate of 12.5 percent, in every year, because the walk initialises to it before any threshold is tested.", "The first tier''s rate, but only in the years the project is profitable, since a royalty is not charged against a loss."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The published case pays 18.0299 million USD on gross revenue of 144.2389 in year 1, an implied 0.125000, and 177.3774 over a life whose present value at 10 percent is negative 111.7254.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 2, 'Three probe decks priced at 49.990000, 50.000000 and 50.010000 USD per bbl run against tiers of 0 and 50 USD per bbl. Which tier owns the threshold itself?', '["The tier above it. The comparison is greater than or equal to, so 49.990000 pays 0.075000 while 50.000000 and 50.010000 both pay 0.100000.", "The tier below it, so a price sitting exactly on 50 pays 0.075000 and only a price above it moves to the upper rate.", "Neither, because the engine treats a price equal to a threshold as ambiguous and holds the rate of the year before.", "It depends on the deck, since a threshold reached by a price multiplier is treated differently from one reached by a deck price."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The three probes read royalties of 14.9214, 19.8989 and 19.9025 million USD on almost identical revenue, which is what makes the ownership of the threshold visible.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 3, 'The walk that picks a royalty tier keeps no memory of the tier it chose the year before. What follows on a deck whose price falls back?', '["The rate holds at the highest tier the price has ever reached, in the way a real sliding scale ratchets once a threshold has been passed.", "The rate is averaged across the tiers the deck has visited, which is what keeps a royalty line smooth across a step in the price deck.", "The rate cannot fall, because the walk initialises each year to the rate it finished the year before and only tests the tiers above it.", "The rate can move up one year and down the next, since the tier is chosen fresh from that year''s own price."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The teaching field pays an implied 0.075000 through year 5 and 0.100000 from year 6 under the PIA tiers, and a deck falling back below 50 USD per bbl would return it to 0.075000 with nothing in the instrument to prevent it.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 4, 'A sweep prints an applied oil price of 50.000000 USD per bbl and an implied royalty rate of 0.075000 against tiers of 0 and 50. What has happened?', '["The engine has a tolerance around a threshold and treats a price within it as not yet reached.", "The printed price is a rounding of a price strictly below the threshold, such as 49.99999999999998, and the implied rate is the measurement of which side the engine was on.", "The tier list was unsorted, so the walk kept a lower tier that appeared later in the list than the one the price had reached.", "The sweep applied the multiplier to revenue but not to the price the royalty function reads, so the two columns disagree."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Read a printed price column as a rounding of the price the engine used, never as the price itself. The rate says which side of the threshold the run was actually on.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 5, 'Angola - Deepwater PSC takes 0.0000 of royalty on the default project while USA - Gulf of Mexico takes 503.7989 million USD. What does a royalty of zero say about the burden of a regime?', '["That the government has given up its first claim on gross revenue, which is why the contractor keeps more of this ledger under Angola than under any other template.", "That the regime has to recover cost faster, since a royalty of zero leaves the whole of gross revenue available to the recovery allowance in year 1.", "That the template is incomplete, because a production sharing contract with no royalty in it cannot express a government''s claim at all.", "Nothing on its own. Angola collects 1200.2886 million USD of government take on that project against 764.5528 for USA, through the profit split instead."]'::jsonb, 3, 'USA - Gulf of Mexico leaves the contractor 980.9313 million USD of net cash flow against 545.1955 for Angola. A royalty is one instrument out of four, and the take is the reading.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 6, 'A sliding royalty selects its tier from the oil price. What happens to the gas and NGL revenue in that year?', '["It is charged at the first tier''s rate, because no gas price reaches a threshold keyed in USD per bbl.", "It is left out of the royalty base, which is why the implied rate on gross revenue reads below the tier''s own rate.", "It is charged at whatever rate the oil price chose, since the rate applies to the whole of gross revenue.", "It is charged at a rate selected from the gas price against the same tier list, so a field can carry two royalty rates in one year."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Only the oil price selects the tier, and the instrument can see nothing else: not cost, not cumulative revenue, not production rate.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 7, 'The teaching field under the PIA template sells 139.6563 million USD in year 1, pays a royalty of 10.4742 and recovers 103.3457 against an 80 percent limit. What was the limit taken as a percent of?', '["Revenue after royalty, because 103.3457 million USD is 80 percent of what is left once the royalty has been taken off the top.", "Gross revenue, so the allowance for the year was 80 percent of 139.6563 million USD and the pool released as much of it as it held.", "The recoverable pool, which is the balance brought forward plus the year''s capex and opex, drawn down at 80 percent of itself.", "Profit oil, since the allowance and the profit share are the two halves of what is left after royalty."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Royalty is taken first and never becomes recoverable cost. The pool closes that year at 341.6581 million USD with the capex of 420.0000 inside it.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 8, 'In year 5 of the default project the ledger spends 23.6264 million USD of opex and no capex, and recovers 83.6287 under a 50 percent limit. How is that possible?', '["The engine recovers the following years'' opex in advance once the capex has been dealt with, which is what the carryforward does.", "Cost recovered is a draw on a historic balance, so a year that spent almost nothing can recover a great deal.", "The 83.6287 million USD includes the uplift the pool earns while waiting.", "The figure is the cumulative recovery to that year rather than the year''s own draw, which is why it is larger than the year''s spend."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Year 1 spent 500.0000 million USD of capex and recovered 118.9951. A reader who treats the recovery column as a cost column double counts the whole of that capex.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 9, 'On the teaching field under the PIA template the pool closes year 8 at 14.5837 million USD and year 9 at 0.0000, and cost recovered falls from 49.9934 in year 8 to 30.4412 in year 9 and 15.3141 in year 10. What is that last figure?', '["The remainder of the year 1 capex of 420.0000 million USD, released on the schedule the engine follows once the early years are settled.", "The 80 percent allowance on that year''s revenue after royalty, which is the binding number in every year of the run.", "A partial draw, since year 10 is the year the pool empties and only part of the allowance can be taken in it.", "That year''s own opex, because the pool is empty and nothing else is left to recover."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Year 9 is the partial draw at 30.4412 million USD. From year 10 the instrument hands back each year''s opex and passes the rest through as profit oil.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 10, 'A published case closes its 25 year life with 2543.7575 million USD still in the unrecovered cost pool. What is that balance worth to the contractor?', '["It is a receivable, so the contractor''s position should be read as the ledger total plus the 2543.7575 million USD still owed.", "It is a tax attribute, since an unrecovered balance shelters the profit share of the years that follow it.", "Nothing. The engine never pays it, nothing carries it past the horizon, and it appears in no other column.", "It is written off against government take on that case."]'::jsonb, 2, 'That case still pays back in year 3 and returns an internal rate of return of 54.6792 percent, because the contractor is also paid through its profit share.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 11, 'On the default project the 80, 90 and 100 percent recovery limits all return a total cost recovered of 941.4436 million USD and a contractor net cash flow of 986.7327. What does the limit decide here?', '["Nothing at all, since the limit is only read in the first year and the default project spends its capex there.", "When the money arrives and not how much of it arrives, because the pool clears inside the life under all three.", "The size of the tax bill, which is the only column a cost recovery limit can reach once the pool has cleared.", "The total recovered, which happens to agree here because the three limits are close together and the project is small."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Only the 50 percent run differs, at 940.4991 recovered and 986.4494 of contractor net cash flow, and only because it leaves 0.9444 million USD unrecovered at the horizon.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 12, 'Capping cost recovery at 5 percent on the test project recovers 306.3022 million USD instead of 2770.6665 and the total tax rises from 1006.6134 to 1745.9227. Why does recovering less cost raise the tax?', '["Cost that cannot be recovered stays inside profit oil, which rises from 3355.3781 to 5819.7423 million USD, and profit oil is what the tax base is cut from.", "The tighter limit lengthens the life of the pool, and the engine charges a carrying cost on an unrecovered balance through the tax line.", "The unrecovered cost is added back to the base as a disallowed deduction, in the way a tax authority treats a cost it will not accept.", "The tax rate rises with the limit, because a regime that recovers less is compensating the government for a longer wait."]'::jsonb, 0, 'If cost recovery were a deduction, less recovery would mean a smaller base. It means a larger one, and government take rises from 1881.7626 to 2621.0719 million USD.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 13, 'Two projects have identical lifetime revenue and lifetime cost totals, one producing them quickly and the other slowly. What does the R factor read on each at the close of the life?', '["A larger value on the fast project, since the ratio is built from cumulatives that already contain the current year and the fast one banks its revenue sooner.", "A larger value on the slow project, because its cost side stops growing while the revenue side keeps accruing to the end of the horizon.", "The same value on both, because revenue in year 1 and revenue in year 25 enter the numerator at face value.", "Values that cannot be compared, since the ratio is defined only against a tier list and two projects rarely carry the same one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The R factor is blind to time value, and it ignores royalty and tax as well, so it is a measure of the project and never of the contractor''s own position.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 14, 'The tranche a ratio selects carries a contractor split. Which part of the ledger does that split reach?', '["Profit oil alone. Cost oil is not split, royalty is not split, and the government''s share of profit oil is one minus the contractor''s.", "Profit oil and cost oil together, since both are shares of revenue after royalty and the tranche divides that whole quantity.", "Profit oil and then the tax base, because the split is applied a second time to the contractor''s share when the tax is charged.", "The whole of gross revenue, with the royalty taken out of the government''s side of the split afterwards."]'::jsonb, 0, 'There is no signature bonus, no production bonus and no state participation line, so a tranche of 0.300000 on profit oil of 80.0217 million USD leaves the contractor 24.0065.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 15, 'The tranche for a year is chosen from a ratio that already contains that year''s own revenue and cost. What does that rule out?', '["The use of the first tier as a default, because a ratio that includes the current year has always reached at least one threshold by the time it is read.", "A crossing that lands between two printed rows, since a ratio computed from the current year takes a value on the threshold itself in the year it steps.", "A split that steps back up late in life, because a running total that includes the current year can only ever grow from one row to the next.", "Any lag. There is no averaging, no memory of last year''s tranche and no proration across a crossing."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Nothing is interpolated and nothing is smoothed. On the fall back case the ratio peaks at 2.972625 in year 11 and is down to 2.494796 by year 23, so the split does step back up.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 16, 'On the published crossing case the split falls from 0.600000 in year 2 to 0.400000 in year 3, with R factors of 1.383252 and 1.846809. Which threshold caused it?', '["The 1.0 threshold, reached in year 3, which is where the golden''s own note dates the first step of the split.", "The 1.6 threshold. Crossing 1.0 happened between years 1 and 2 and changed nothing, because 60 percent was already in force.", "The 2.5 threshold, which the ratio passes in year 3 on its way to a peak above 3 later in the life.", "Both the 1.0 and the 1.6 thresholds in the same year, since the walk applies every tier it has reached and the last one wins."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The golden''s note misattributes its own step. Quote a golden''s numbers and never a golden''s prose, because no mechanical check reads an association.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 17, 'On the published fall back case the R factor peaks at 2.972625 in year 11 and reads 2.494796 in year 23. What does the split do in year 23?', '["It holds at 0.300000, because a tranche once entered is retained for the rest of the life the way a real contract ratchets.", "It falls again to the lowest split on the list, since any movement in the ratio late in life is read as further maturity.", "It steps back up from 0.300000 to 0.400000, because the ratio has fallen below 2.5 and the walk now stops at the 1.6 tier.", "It becomes undefined, because profit oil of 3.1570 million USD is too small for the engine to select a tranche against."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Cumulative revenue grows more slowly every year while cumulative cost never stops growing, so the ratio turns. Nothing in the engine ratchets and nothing warns.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 18, 'The tier walk keeps the split of every tier whose threshold the ratio has reached, one after another. What survives the walk?', '["Whichever qualifying tier came last in the list, which is the highest threshold reached only while the list is sorted.", "Whichever qualifying tier carries the highest threshold, since the walk compares each tier against the one it would replace.", "Whichever qualifying tier carries the lowest split, because the engine resolves a tie in the government''s favour.", "Whichever tier the previous year selected, unless a new threshold has been reached since."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Every shipped template is sorted, so nothing is wrong today. The engine does not sort the list, does not check that thresholds ascend and raises no error, and no gate in the app checks the order.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 19, 'Which instruments in this model are charged on gross revenue?', '["The royalty alone, since every tax in the stack is charged on the contractor''s profit share.", "The royalty and the minimum tax, and nothing else.", "The royalty, the minimum tax and the corporate income tax, which between them are the three charges taken before cost recovery.", "The royalty and the resource rent tax, because the uplift makes the second of them a charge on production rather than on profit."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The other two taxes are charged on the profit share. That is why a 10 percent minimum can collect 700.1194 million USD in a case whose year 1 profit oil is 0.0000.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 20, 'In the case where the minimum tax binds, year 3 has gross revenue of 678.3744 million USD, profit oil of 499.6589 and a tax of 67.8374, with the corporate income tax set to 0. What is the tax?', '["A 10 percent charge on the profit share, reduced by the cost still sitting in the recovery pool in that year of the life.", "The larger of the corporate income tax and the minimum, which on a rate of 0 means the profit share was charged at a residual rate.", "A blend of the minimum tax and the profits taxes, weighted by how far the floor stands above them in that year.", "A 10 percent minimum tax on gross revenue."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The tax charged is the larger of the profits taxes and the minimum, never their sum. Here the profits taxes are 0.0000 so the floor is the whole charge, and it follows revenue down.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 21, 'Isolated on the default project, a corporate income tax of 34 percent collects 502.1091 million USD over the life and a resource rent tax of 40 percent collects 62.5794. What makes the second so much smaller?', '["Its base is the profit share less 20 percent of the whole 500.0000 million USD of capex, and that subtraction is made in every one of the 25 years.", "Its base is the profit share less the cost recovered in the year, which on this project comes to 941.4436 million USD over the life.", "It is charged only in the years after the recovery pool has cleared, and the pool clears late on this project.", "It is charged on the government''s share of profit oil, which is the smaller side of the split on this regime."]'::jsonb, 0, 'At the default uplift the relief over the life is five times the whole capex, which the engine''s own comment calls a screening approximation. The charge is back to 0.0000 by year 9.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 22, 'A sweep of the resource rent tax uplift reports a government take of 331.2721 million USD at the default 20 percent. Why is that not the regime''s government take on that project?', '["The sweep reports take before royalty, which has to be added back before the figure can be compared with a published one.", "The sweep runs a different project, so its columns are not comparable with the default project at all.", "The sweep sets the template''s own corporate income tax of 34 percent to zero in every row, so the take it prints is one instrument short.", "The sweep reports the discounted take, which is why it is smaller than the published figure on the same regime."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The published take for that regime on the default project is 833.3812 million USD. Sections 10 and 11 carry the stack; the sweep carries an isolation.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 23, 'By what route does the 500.0000 million USD of capex on the default project enter the tax computation?', '["Through cost recovery, which returns the capital and reduces the base by the same amount in the year it is returned.", "Through the resource rent tax uplift and no other, and four of the six templates set that rate to 0.", "Through a capital allowance spread over the 25 year horizon, which is what the uplift percent expresses.", "Through the royalty, which is charged on gross revenue and so reduces every quantity the tax base is built from."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The base is the profit share and recovery has been settled in cash, so no part of the 941.4436 million USD recovered appears in the tax computation.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 24, 'On the test project USA - Gulf of Mexico collects 616.4851 million USD of tax inside a government take of 1929.2089, and Nigeria - PIA (2021) collects 389.4896 inside 3372.6496. Which regime is the heavier?', '["USA, on the tax, since tax is the only column in which a government''s claim on a contractor is actually collected.", "Neither, because the two columns disagree and a comparison can only be made on regimes whose orders agree.", "Nigeria, on the take. The tax column measures one instrument and the take measures the regime.", "USA, because a higher tax against a lower take means more of its claim is being taken through instruments the take does not include."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two columns point in opposite directions on the same ledger, because the government''s share of profit oil never passes through the tax line.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 25, 'On identical cash flows the mid year present value at 20 percent is 176.3275 million USD against a year end 160.9642. What is the relation?', '["A fixed premium of about 15 million USD, which is the half year of discounting the mid year convention gives back on the first row.", "A ratio that grows with the size of the ledger, so a larger field shows a larger gap between the two conventions.", "A ratio set by the shape of the decline, since a steeper profile puts more of its cash in the years the two conventions treat differently.", "The square root of one plus the rate, 1.095445 at 20 percent."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The relation is pinned by a parity gate and depends on the rate alone: 1.024695 at 5 percent, 1.048809 at 10 and 1.140175 at 30.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 26, 'A swept table of present values reports 980.9313 million USD at a rate of 0 percent. What must that value equal?', '["The undiscounted total contractor net cash flow of the same ledger, which is the cheapest check there is on a discounted number.", "The present value at the project''s own rate, since a rate of zero is read as an instruction to use the project default.", "The total government take, because at a rate of zero the two sides of the ledger are no longer separated by timing.", "Nothing in particular, since a rate of zero is a degenerate case the function handles separately."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A rate of zero simply makes every divisor one. A top row that does not match the ledger total is reading different flows, since at a rate of zero every discounting convention returns the same sum.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 27, 'Two published cases report an internal rate of return of 0.0000 percent, one worth 25.6198 million USD at 10 percent and one worth negative 16.5289. What do the two zeros mean?', '["Both projects break even exactly, which is what a rate of zero reports and why the two present values straddle zero.", "One project never loses, because its flows never change sign, and the other never wins, because its only root is negative.", "One is a genuine root and the other is the guard, so only the case with the positive present value can be read as a rate.", "Both are the doubling bracket collapsing to its lower bound, which is what the search returns when it cannot bracket a root."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The function prints the same four characters for both, and a reader who takes 0.0000 for break even has it backwards in both directions at once.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 28, 'Flows of negative 1 then 2000 return 102400.0000 percent from the solver. What should a reader do with that figure?', '["Quote it as the rate, since the golden records the same value and the two therefore agree.", "Halve it, because the bracket the doubling reached always overshoots the root by a factor of two.", "Treat it as the top of the doubling search rather than a rate, because the true root is 199900.0000 percent.", "Read it as an error code, since a solver that fails to converge in 80 bisections signals the failure through the return value."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The tell is a suspiciously round number where a rate should be. A rate that came out of 80 bisections looks like 13.0662 percent.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 29, 'A comparison is filtered to regimes returning more than a threshold rate. What does that filter remove?', '["Only the projects whose present value at a rate of zero is negative, which is the second of the solver''s two guards.", "The projects whose rate is a reported bracket rather than a root, because a bound is always above any sensible threshold.", "Nothing that matters, provided the threshold sits below the lowest genuine rate returned.", "The hopeless projects and the ones that never needed capital together, since both report 0.0000 percent."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The case with capex of 20000 reports 0.0000 percent on a contractor net cash flow of negative 15724.0151 million USD, and a case with no outlay at all reports the same figure.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 30, 'The published case with flows of negative 100 then 121 returns 21.0000 percent. What does that case pin?', '["The convention, because under year end discounting one plus the rate is 1.21 and the root is therefore 21 percent.", "The bracketing, since the doubling from 100 percent has to step past the root before any bisection can begin.", "The precision of the 80 bisections, which is what lets the engine and the golden agree to four decimals.", "The sign change guard, since a vector holding one negative and one positive entry is the smallest case with a root in it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A rate is a year end rate here, and a mid year convention on those same two flows would not solve to 21.0000 percent.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 31, 'On the teaching field every regime shares an opex column starting at 25.0037 million USD and totalling 390.2137, and a capex of 420.0000 in year 1. What follows from that?', '["The regimes cannot be compared, since one cost profile cannot carry each contract''s operating terms.", "Any difference between two regimes on that field is fiscal by construction, because no regime touches the cost columns.", "The cost recovery limit is the only instrument that can separate them, because it is the only one that reads the cost columns at all.", "The comparison is only valid at the field''s own discount rate of 12 percent, since cost held constant biases every other rate."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Reading the columns no regime touches first is what makes the rest of the comparison legible. Capex is 0.0000 in every year after the first.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 32, 'Brazil - Concession charges 34 and 40 percent on the teaching field and collects 109.3752 million USD of tax. USA - Gulf of Mexico charges 21 percent alone and collects 45.8808, and Angola - Deepwater PSC charges 25 and 50 and collects 100.4364. What ranks the collections?', '["The number of instruments each regime carries, since two profits taxes will always collect more than one.", "The royalty each regime takes first, because a larger royalty leaves a smaller profit share for the rates to reach.", "The base each rate is charged on, which is why the rates and the collections do not run in the same order.", "The discount rate, since the tax column on a 25 year ledger is dominated by the years the discounting weights most heavily."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Brazil carries the highest pair of rates and does not collect the most per point of rate. Naming the base before the rate is the habit that survives into the comparison.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 33, 'On the teaching field Angola - Deepwater PSC recovers 618.0991 million USD and closes with 192.1146 still in the pool, while Brazil - Concession recovers 790.8868 and closes at 19.3270. What is the closing pool telling a reader?', '["It is money the contractor spent that the ledger never paid back, so a recovery total should never be read without it.", "It is the cost still to be recovered in the years after the horizon, which the model settles when the licence is handed back.", "It is the difference between the two regimes'' cost recovery limits, expressed in the currency of the ledger.", "It is the balance on which the resource rent tax uplift is computed, which is why Angola''s tax is the larger of the two."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Angola runs a 50 percent limit and Brazil a 100 percent one. The limit costs the contractor time until the pool fails to clear, and then it costs money.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 34, 'The teaching field summary is sorted by contractor net present value descending and Brazil - Concession is first at negative 5.8662 million USD. Government take runs the other way, 316.7898 for Angola against 232.9950 for Brazil. Which order is right?', '["The take order, since a present value that is negative for every regime cannot rank anything and should not be sorted on at all.", "The present value order, because the summary''s own sort is the engine''s verdict and the take column is a derived quantity beside it.", "Neither, because the two disagree, and a ranking that reverses under a second column is evidence that the comparison was mis-specified.", "Both, and they are orders of different things."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The best regime for the contractor on that field has a present value of negative 5.8662 million USD. Being least bad is a real answer, and it needs its label saying so.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 35, 'Three of the six templates split profit oil on the R factor. What does a year in which the contractor''s tranche is 0.300000 do to the tax base?', '["It leaves the whole of profit oil taxable, since the tax is charged before the split and the split then divides what is left of it.", "It leaves 0.300000 of profit oil taxable and charges the government''s share at the same rate, which the take column reports on its own.", "It leaves only the contractor''s 0.300000 of profit oil taxable, because the government''s share of profit oil never passes through the tax line.", "It leaves the whole of profit oil taxable but at 0.300000 of the rate, which is how a split is expressed inside the stack."]'::jsonb, 2, 'In year 11 of the default project a tranche of 0.300000 on 80.0217 million USD of profit oil leaves a base of 24.0065, which is how Ghana - Deepwater collects 230.9340 over the life at 35 percent while Generic Royalty/Tax collects 422.8854 at 30.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 36, 'In year 3 of the published crossing case profit oil jumps from 46.2528 to 179.0750 million USD while the contractor''s split falls from 0.600000 to 0.400000. Is the contractor worse off?', '["No. A larger share of a much smaller quantity is less money than a smaller share of a much larger one, and the pool clearing is what released the profit oil.", "Yes, because the tranche crossing takes a fifth of the contractor''s profit oil away in the year it happens.", "Yes, because a split falling in the same year that profit oil rises means the government captured the whole of the increase.", "It cannot be told from those figures, because the split applies to cost oil as well and that column is not shown."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A split rate and a share of money are different quantities and can move in opposite directions in one row. Cost recovery cleared and the tranche crossed in the same year.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 37, 'Two runs of the same regime on the same project differ only in the resource rent tax uplift, one taking the default and one set to 0. Present value at 10 percent is 961.7819 million USD against 473.7983 and the rate of return 107.2817 percent against 72.4998. What moved them?', '["The discount rate, since the two runs are reported at different rates and only one of them is the project''s own.", "One field left out of the inputs, which took its documented default of 20 percent and sheltered part of the base in every year.", "The cost recovery limit, which is the only input that can move a present value and a rate of return together on one project.", "A minimum tax that bound in one run and not the other, which changed the years the charge falls in and therefore the discounting."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Total tax is 1638.5664 million USD against 2607.5549. A default that decides half a present value is worth reading in the inputs and not in the output.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 38, 'One published run returns 2.9910 percent, reaches payback only in year 12 and closes at negative 111.7254 million USD of present value, and it still hands over 177.3774 of royalty. What do those readings show together?', '["That the royalty is the reason the rate of return is so low, since an instrument charged before cost is what a marginal project cannot carry.", "That the rate of return is the binding reading, because a charge on revenue is waived in any year a project earns less than its discount rate.", "That the two readings belong to different runs, since a project returning a positive rate of return cannot also carry a negative present value.", "A royalty is charged on gross revenue whether or not the project makes money."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The royalty collected is more than twice the contractor''s whole lifetime cash flow of 80.8085 million USD. The instrument sees a price, picks a rate and charges gross revenue.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 39, 'A test project run at 20000 of capex closes with 15724.0151 million USD in the pool, reports a contractor net cash flow of negative 15724.0151 and an internal rate of return of 0.0000 percent. Which guard produced the rate?', '["The sign change check, since a ledger with no positive contractor cash flow in any year never changes sign.", "The bracketing step, which returned its lower bound because the present value was negative across the whole search.", "The check on the present value at a rate of zero, which is not above zero here, so the engine reports 0 rather than a negative rate.", "None of them. The rate is a genuine root, and a project that recovers exactly its outlay returns zero."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Present value at 10 percent is negative 15453.8510 million USD, government take is 875.1492 and all of it is royalty, because royalty does not wait for cost to be recovered.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 40, 'On the teaching field under the PIA template the implied royalty rate steps from 0.075000 to 0.100000 in year 6, and the R factor crosses 1.0 in the same year, from 0.997329 to 1.136133. Are those the same event?', '["Yes. Reaching payout is what moves a field into the upper royalty tier, which is why both columns change in the same row.", "No. The royalty moved because the deck steps to 65 USD per bbl in year 6, and the ratio crossed because cumulative revenue overtook cumulative cost.", "Yes, because both instruments read the applied oil price, and a deck step therefore moves the royalty tier and the ratio together.", "No, and the royalty step is the earlier of the two, since a price event reaches the ledger a year before a cumulative one can."]'::jsonb, 1, 'One is a price event and the other is a cumulative one, and this field is the only teaching case where a royalty tier changes inside the life.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 41, 'Every regime on the teaching field reports an R factor payout year of 6, while payback years run 6 for Brazil - Concession, 7 for Generic Royalty/Tax and 8 for Angola - Deepwater PSC. Why does one agree across regimes and the other not?', '["Payout is the first year the R factor is above 1.0 and the ratio is net of nothing fiscal, while payback is the first year cumulative contractor net cash flow turns positive and that is net of royalty, tax and the split.", "Payout is computed from the summary and payback from the ledger, and the two are produced by different functions on different projects.", "Payout is rounded to the nearest year across the six regimes, while payback is reported exactly as each ledger returns it.", "Payback moves because each regime carries a different cost recovery limit, and payout does not because the limit is applied after the ratio."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The R factor responds only to production, price and cost. Two measures that agree on one project encourage the habit of treating them as one, and they are not.'),
  ('fiscal', 'intermediate', 'final', null, 42, 'What does this sandbox refuse to be?', '["A comparison of the shapes of regimes, which requires the real fiscal codes rather than four instruments on one ledger.", "A model of the timing of cost recovery, since the pool it carries has no schedule and cannot represent a real recovery profile.", "A ledger for a field with more than one product stream, because only the oil price selects a royalty tier.", "A second fiscal truth, and a source of any number quotable as a tax liability."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The module''s single source of truth for Nigerian fiscal math is the Petroleum Economics Studio engine. A comparison of shapes is worth a great deal; a number lifted out of it and quoted as a liability is worth nothing.');
