-- ============================================================================
-- EC2 PROFESSIONAL DEEP SEED: Fiscal Regime Design, intermediate tier.
--
-- Six modules, 26 lessons, 132 questions (15 per module bank plus a 42
-- question final exam). The structure row is GENERATED from the tier's
-- manifest.json rather than hand typed.
--
-- What this tier owns: THE INSTRUMENTS THAT MOVE. The sliding-scale royalty
-- and its price thresholds; the recoverable pool, the cost recovery limit
-- and the carryforward; the R factor as a ratio of cumulatives, the tranche
-- it selects, crossing a tranche and falling back below one, and the hazard
-- in tier order; the tax stack, its base, corporate income tax, resource
-- rent tax with its annual capital uplift, and the minimum tax under a
-- maximum; year-end discounting, the square-root parity with the mid-year
-- screening engine, and the bisection internal rate of return.
--
-- EVERY TIER SELECTION IN THIS ENGINE IS A STEP AND A LIST WALK. Both the
-- royalty and the profit split start at the FIRST tier's value and keep the
-- value of every tier whose threshold has been reached, walking the list in
-- ORDER. Nothing is interpolated between tiers, the first tier's rate
-- applies below every threshold rather than a zero, and "the last tier in
-- list order whose threshold is reached" equals "the highest threshold
-- reached" only while the list is sorted. Every shipped template is sorted.
--
-- THE TRAP THE TIER IS BUILT ON. The R factor is cumulative revenue over
-- cumulative cost, INCLUDING the current year, computed before the split is
-- chosen. A reader who takes the year's own revenue over the year's own
-- cost gets a different number in every single year. And because it is a
-- ratio of cumulatives it is NOT monotone: late in life revenue declines
-- while opex keeps accruing, the ratio can fall back below a threshold it
-- had passed, and the contractor's split steps back UP. Real R factor
-- contracts usually ratchet; this one does not and nothing in it says so.
--
-- THE ENGINE FACT THIS TIER OWNS. The tax base is the contractor's profit
-- share and nothing else, because cost is compensated through cost recovery
-- and is not deducted a second time. An earlier version halved opex against
-- the base; the halving was invented and was removed. The resource rent tax
-- then subtracts a capital uplift from that base in EVERY one of the 25
-- years, so at the default 20 percent the relief over the life is five
-- times the capex, which the engine calls a screening approximation in its
-- own comment and the parameter name does not.
--
-- The only numbers that travel from this tier to the next are the per-regime
-- ledgers and the NPV and IRR read off them.
-- ============================================================================

values ('fiscal', 'intermediate', '{"modules":[{"key":"m01-the-sliding-scale","title":"The Sliding Scale","lesson_keys":["l01-royalty-that-moves-with-price","l02-the-tier-that-is-chosen","l03-a-deck-that-crosses-a-threshold","l04-below-every-threshold"]},{"key":"m02-cost-recovery","title":"Cost Recovery","lesson_keys":["l01-the-recoverable-pool","l02-the-limit-and-its-base","l03-the-carryforward","l04-when-cost-is-never-recovered","l05-what-cost-recovery-is-not"]},{"key":"m03-the-r-factor","title":"The R Factor","lesson_keys":["l01-a-ratio-of-cumulatives","l02-the-tranche-the-ratio-selects","l03-crossing-a-tranche","l04-falling-back","l05-the-order-of-the-tiers"]},{"key":"m04-the-tax-stack","title":"The Tax Stack","lesson_keys":["l01-the-base-is-the-profit-share","l02-corporate-income-tax","l03-resource-rent-tax-and-its-uplift","l04-the-minimum-tax-and-the-maximum","l05-what-the-stack-leaves-out"]},{"key":"m05-rate-and-return","title":"Rate and Return","lesson_keys":["l01-year-end-discounting","l02-the-parity-with-mid-year","l03-irr-by-bisection","l04-when-irr-returns-zero"]},{"key":"m06-the-professional-reading","title":"The Professional Reading","lesson_keys":["l01-the-story-so-far","l02-working-the-capstone","l03-onward"]}]}'::jsonb, 1)
